Harry observed the Pi Daemon canary successfully on ms-dev-4 in the Pi Daemon browser dashboard. Runtime pi-daemon generation/agent g2bux59b2snewhqn was reachable and the first-party host filesystem adapter responded successfully, but earlier tool calls intermittently failed with the exact error:

`Host tool adapter request queue is full`

The canary ultimately completed, so this is adapter capacity/backpressure rather than basic reachability. Current request parallelism or bounded queue capacity is too low for ordinary concurrent agent tool traffic.

Required investigation and acceptance:
- record configured queue capacity, worker concurrency, active/in-flight counts, queue wait, rejection count, and per-method latency in the dashboard/structured receipt;
- distinguish a genuinely exhausted bounded resource from a stale slot/leaked permit;
- make parallelism and queue capacity typed/configurable with safe host-aware defaults;
- prefer bounded waiting with explicit retry metadata over immediate opaque rejection when capacity should clear quickly;
- preserve hard memory/CPU/fd bounds and avoid unbounded queues;
- add a concurrent host-tool fixture that exceeds the old limit, proves no lost requests or leaked permits, and returns deterministic success or structured retryable backpressure;
- verify on an ms-dev-4-class host under the same harmless canary workload.

Observed 2026-08-08 by Harry in the ms-dev-4 Pi Daemon dashboard. Canary itself succeeded and made no source/provider/config/service mutation.


