Determine whether the missing tools are an implementation gap or configuration/materialization gap, then provide profile-authorized parity:

- materialize the effective managed profile/tool catalog into Pi Daemon sessions rather than defaulting to filesystem-only tools;
- expose first-party Cacophony messaging/coordination tools required by worker profiles, with exact project/agent authorization and durable audit identity;
- expose Bash only when the effective profile grants it, rooted in the exact managed checkout with checkout-guard, environment, timeout, output bounds, and process cleanup semantics matching managed Pi;
- never give ambient shell or cross-project authority to a filesystem-only/untrusted session merely for parity;
- preserve the same tool identities/capabilities when a logical session moves between web, local TUI, and remote TUI clients;
- surface effective tool inventory, source/profile generation, omitted-tool reasons, and authorization scope in Pi Daemon dashboard/status;
- add fixtures for filesystem-only profile, worker profile with caco_msg and Bash, unauthorized tool refusal, cross-surface continuation, restart/refresh materialization, and stale catalog invalidation;
- verify the canary can send a harmless project message and run a bounded read-only checkout command after authorization, without claiming work or changing files/provider/config/services.

Coordinate with host-adapter queue bead bd-d6131f so broader tool exposure does not worsen saturation without configured parallelism/backpressure.

Pi Daemon canary succeeded on ms-dev-4 for generation g2bux59b2snewhqn: first-party host filesystem adapter was reachable and responded successfully, with no work claim or mutation. During earlier tool calls the ms-dev-4 Pi Daemon dashboard showed: Host tool adapter request queue is full.

Required fix and acceptance:

- reproduce the concurrent canary/tool-call burst and identify current queue capacity, active-worker limit, per-invocation cost, and rejection threshold;
- make queue capacity and execution parallelism typed/configurable with safe host-aware defaults rather than hard-coded low limits;
- raise effective concurrency enough for realistic parallel agent/tool bursts while retaining a bounded queue, cancellation, deadlines, and resource protection;
- distinguish queue wait/backpressure from terminal rejection; do not silently drop or duplicate requests;
- apply fairness so one noisy session cannot starve other agents or host adapter classes;
- expose queue depth, active workers, capacity, wait latency, rejections, and saturation duration in the existing Pi Daemon dashboard/health surface;
- add burst/stress fixtures covering successful parallel filesystem calls, bounded overload behavior, cancellation, response ordering/identity, and recovery after saturation;
- measure before/after throughput and p95 wait/error rate on ms-dev-4-class resources.

Do not merely make the queue unbounded or set an arbitrarily huge worker count; preserve host stability and deterministic backpressure semantics.


